    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "1719178e_6712f8ce",
        "filename": "chip/stm32/usb_spi.h",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 239,
      "author": {
        "id": 1347380
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-06-03T21:01:43Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Half duplex mode has not been enabled on the STM32 based USB SPI platforms, so they perform one write transfer and one read transfer as separate operations.\n\nIf half duplex mode is enabled, this would be a significant concern since the lower level SPI implementation may or may not work depending on how data is transferred to the registered and read from it during the transaction. If it\u0027s implementation has write a block of data to a registers out and then read a register value to the target address, it\u0027ll probably work, but may fail on some platforms.\n\n\ncr50/chip/g/usb_spi.c does support a SPI_READBACK_ALL case when you send it 255 for the read_count, but it also shares the same buffer for both calls.\n\nif (read_count \u003d\u003d 255) {\n\t/* Handle simultaneously clocked RX and TX */\n\tread_count \u003d SPI_READBACK_ALL;\n\tread_length \u003d write_count;\n} else {\n...\nspi_transaction(SPI_FLASH_DEVICE,\n\t\tconfig-\u003ebuffer + HEADER_SIZE,\n\t\twrite_count,\n\t\tconfig-\u003ebuffer + HEADER_SIZE,\n\t\tread_count));\n\nI suspect right now that there are currently no hosts that actually use half duplex mode. Flashrom is the only host that I am aware of and it\u0027s implementation of the protocol doesn\u0027t support it in raiden_debug_spi.c where the read_count is directly passed to a memcpy without appropriate conversion if it did support half duplex operation.",
      "parentUuid": "1153a91a_3b2aa2ab",
